<article class="env-corollary" id="06RV">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06RV">Corollary <span data-tag="06RV">4.7.7.24</span>.</a> Let $n$ be an integer and let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $n$-category. Then, for every diagram $f: K \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, the slice and coslice $\infty $-categories $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{f/}$ and $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{/f}$ are $n$-categories. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      If $n \leq -2$, then $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is isomorphic to $\Delta ^0$ (Example <a data-tag="057P" href="/tag/057P">4.7.7.11</a>) and the result is trivial. We may therefore assume without loss of generality that $n \geq -1$. Construction <a data-tag="02GH" href="/tag/02GH">4.6.4.15</a> supplies functors 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{/f} \hookrightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\vec{\times }_{ \operatorname{Fun}(K,\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) } \{ f\}  \quad \quad \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{f/} \hookrightarrow \{ f\}  \vec{\times }_{ \operatorname{Fun}(K, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) } \operatorname{\mathcal{C}} \]
</div>
<p>
       which are monomorphisms of simplicial sets (Remark <a data-tag="01KT" href="/tag/01KT">4.6.4.17</a>). By virtue of Remark <a data-tag="057V" href="/tag/057V">4.7.7.17</a>, it will suffice to show that the oriented fiber products $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\vec{\times }_{ \operatorname{Fun}(K,\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) } \{ f\} $ and $\{ f\}  \vec{\times }_{\operatorname{Fun}(K, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) } \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ are $n$-categories, which follows from Proposition <a data-tag="0581" href="/tag/0581">4.7.7.22</a> and Corollary <a data-tag="0582" href="/tag/0582">4.7.7.23</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
